." ln(1)
.TH LN 1 "March 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ln \- make links between files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ln
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI TARGET LINK_NAME
.br
.B ln
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI TARGET
.br
.B ln
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI TARGET ... DIRECTORY
.br
.B ln
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.BI "-t " DIRECTORY " TARGET ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ln
creates hard links by default, and symbolic links with
.BR -s .
.PP
Destination selection follows POSIX form rules with GNU extensions:
.TP
- one source + non-directory destination: create that single link
.TP
- multiple sources: final operand must be an existing directory
.TP
-
.B -t
forces destination-directory mode
.TP
-
.B -T
disables destination-directory interpretation
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -f , " --force"
Remove existing destination before linking.
.TP
.BR -i , " --interactive"
Prompt before replacing an existing destination.
.TP
.BR -s , " --symbolic"
Create symbolic links.
.TP
.BR -L , " --logical"
When hard-linking and source is a symlink, link the referent.
.TP
.BR -P , " --physical"
When hard-linking and source is a symlink, link the symlink itself.
.TP
.BR -h , " -n" , " --no-dereference"
BSD semantics: do not follow destination symlink-to-directory.
.TP
.B -F
BSD semantics: with
.B -s
and directory destination collision, remove the directory first.
.TP
.B -w
With
.B -s
warn if source path does not currently exist.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Print one line per created link.
.TP
.BR -b , " --backup" [ =METHOD ]
Create destination backups before replacement.
.TP
.BR -S , " --suffix=" SUFFIX
Set backup suffix.
.TP
.BR -t , " --target-directory=" DIR
Use
.I DIR
as destination directory.
.TP
.BR -T , " --no-target-directory"
Never treat final operand as destination directory.
.TP
.BR -r , " --relative"
With
.B -s
compute a relative symlink target from destination directory.
.TP
.BR -d , " --directory"
Attempt hard links to directories when policy allows.
.TP
.B --help
Display help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
All requested links were created.
.TP
1
One or more link operations failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
ln file link
Create a hard link.
.TP
ln -s lib.so.1 lib.so
Create a symbolic link.
.TP
ln -s -r /opt/tree/lib/libfoo.so /opt/tree/bin/libfoo.so
Create a relative symlink target.
.TP
ln -t stage/usr/bin tool1 tool2
Link multiple files into a destination directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR link (2),
.BR readlink (2),
.BR unlink (2),
.BR stat (2)
